# TCP/IP Server in C

Here's a complete implementation of a TCP/IP server in C that listens for incoming connections and echoes back any data it receives:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>

#define PORT 8080
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

int main() {
    int server_fd, new_socket;
    struct sockaddr_in address;
    int opt = 1;
    int addrlen = sizeof(address);
    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE] = {0};

    // Create socket file descriptor
    if ((server_fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0)) == 0) {
        perror("socket failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Set socket options to reuse address and port
    if (setsockopt(server_fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR | SO_REUSEPORT, &opt, sizeof(opt))) {
        perror("setsockopt");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    address.sin_family = AF_INET;
    address.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;  // Accept connections from any interface
    address.sin_port = htons(PORT);

    // Bind the socket to the specified port
    if (bind(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&address, sizeof(address)) < 0) {
        perror("bind failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Start listening for incoming connections
    if (listen(server_fd, 3) < 0) {
        perror("listen");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    printf("Server listening on port %d...\n", PORT);

    while (1) {
        // Accept incoming connection
        if ((new_socket = accept(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&address, (socklen_t*)&addrlen)) < 0) {
            perror("accept");
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }

        // Print client information
        char client_ip[INET_ADDRSTRLEN];
        inet_ntop(AF_INET, &(address.sin_addr), client_ip, INET_ADDRSTRLEN);
        printf("Connection accepted from %s:%d\n", client_ip, ntohs(address.sin_port));

        // Handle client communication
        while (1) {
            memset(buffer, 0, BUFFER_SIZE);
            int valread = read(new_socket, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE);

            if (valread <= 0) {
                // Client disconnected or error occurred
                printf("Client disconnected\n");
                break;
            }

            printf("Received: %s", buffer);

            // Echo back the received data
            send(new_socket, buffer, strlen(buffer), 0);
            printf("Echoed back to client\n");
        }

        // Close the connection socket
        close(new_socket);
        printf("Connection closed\n");
    }

    // Close the server socket (though we never reach here in this example)
    close(server_fd);
    return 0;
}
```

## How to Compile and Run

1. Save this code to a file named `tcp_server.c`
2. Compile it with:
   ```bash
   gcc tcp_server.c -o tcp_server
   ```
3. Run the server:
   ```bash
   ./tcp_server
   ```

## Key Features

1. Creates a TCP socket using `socket()`
2. Sets socket options to allow address reuse
3. Binds to all available network interfaces on port 8080
4. Listens for incoming connections
5. Accepts connections in a loop
6. Reads data from clients and echoes it back
7. Handles multiple sequential connections (though not concurrently)

## Testing the Server

You can test this server using:
- `telnet localhost 8080` from another terminal
- `nc localhost 8080` (netcat)
- Or write a simple TCP client program

## Notes

- This is a simple single-threaded server that handles one connection at a time.
- For a production server, you would want to:
  - Add error handling for all system calls
  - Implement proper signal handling
  - Use threads or forking to handle multiple clients concurrently
  - Add logging and monitoring
  - Implement proper shutdown procedures

